  [1mA. Global namespace script ([38;5;153msession.js[39m) — recommended.[0m A new [38;5;153msession.js[39m defines one global, [38;5;153mAppSession[39m, with [38;5;153msetUser()
[39m  / [38;5;153mgetUser()[39m / [38;5;153mclear()[39m, backed by [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m and read-through on every [38;5;153mgetUser()[39m (no in-memory cache, so two
  scripts on a page can't disagree). Every page adds one [38;5;153m<script src="session.js">[39m before its own script. [3mBuys:[0m works
  today, under [38;5;153mfile://[39m as well as a server; zero infrastructure; the idiomatic pattern for a no-build multi-page static
  site. [3mCosts:[0m a global, and load-order discipline — a page that forgets the tag fails at runtime, not at build.
  [3mComplexity: trivial.[0m

  [1mB. ES modules.[0m [38;5;153msession.js[39m exports named functions; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
  imports from it. [3mBuys:[0m real module boundaries, no global, explicit dependencies, and it's the same syntax as a bundler
  later. [3mCosts:[0m modules are fetched with CORS, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m from disk stops working — you'd need a local http
  server from then on. Also switches scripts to deferred execution. [3mComplexity: trivial-to-moderate,[0m almost all of it in
  the workflow change, not the code. [3mThis wins if you already serve the app over http and never open the file directly.[0m

  [1mC. Bundler + shared module with [38;5;153msrc/[39m.[0m Add npm tooling (esbuild or similar) and make one module that both the browser
  app and the CommonJS [38;5;153msrc/[39m tree consume. [3mBuys:[0m one module world instead of today's two. [3mCosts:[0m a build step, a
  dependency tree, and a watch/rebuild loop for a six-file static page. [3mComplexity: high.[0m I'd rule this out — nothing in
  the request needs browser/Node code sharing, and YAGNI says don't buy a build system for one store.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m It satisfies every constraint you set with the least machinery, and the upgrade path to B is
  mechanical — convert the functions to exports and add [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m — so choosing A now doesn't cost you B later. I'd
  only lead with B if the file-protocol tradeoff is a non-issue for you.
